President Biden has announced new  immigration policies ahead of his visit  to El Paso this weekend the city has  been struggling to cope with an influx  of migrants seeking asylum in the U.S  the new policies are aimed at reducing  the number of illegal Crossings at the  U.S Mexico border each month up to 30  000 people from four countries Venezuela  Haiti Nicaragua and Cuba will be  accepted if they can prove they have a  financial sponsor in the United States  but those who enter the U.S illegally  may be expelled to Mexico under title 42  a rising number of migrants from Cuba  and Haiti is also arriving by boat in  Florida where another humanitarian  crisis is unfolding Manuel bojorquez has  that part of the story  U.S Coast Guard video shows agents  encountering people on rafts or crowded  boats taking on water  the arrival of more than 300 migrants at  Dry Tortugas a national park in the  Florida Keys this week led to the Park's  temporary closure even cruise ships have  stopped to rescue people at Sea Asylum  Seekers hoping for this  a reunion with family members already in  the U.S like Edie's Fundora from Cuba  who arrived in the keys after a week at  Sea with her two boys  content you're happy they're part of a  recent rise in crossings by sea since  October 1st of 2022 the Customs and  Border Protection Miami sector reports a  400 percent increase in migrant  encounters  many Cubans say they are fleeing  political persecution at the hands of  the communist government but also an  economy that has gone from bad to worse  Haitians are fleeing their Homeland too  as the country descends further into  chaos gang violence and hunger they do  not risk their lives unless what they  leave behind is worse than death Leone  is with Saint law a community resource  center for Haitians in Miami we spoke  with her just as another boat full of  Haitians arrived in Key Largo this week  when your country does not provide you  with the safety the security and staying  in there is worse than death that as  they say they rather go deal with the  dangers of the sea yet for many the  journey ends in deportation and a return  to the very country and conditions from  which they fled for CBS mornings I'm  Manuel bojorquez in the Florida Keys
 

